To install and run the svb program follow the steps described below:

Installation:
===============

* Unpack the tar-file in a suitable place, preferable under you src
  directory. It will generate a svb directory with the source code and the
  necessary assisting files.

* Modify the Makefile to reflect your configuration

* Compile the program with: % make install
			    % rehash

  This will generate the executable and place it in the bin directory
  specified in the Makefile. It will also copy the svbinit script to
  the bin directory.

Use of svb:
=============

  To use svb plot you will have to have your data in a suitable format
in a directory.

* Then do: % svbinit

  This will copy a template input file (svb.in) to your working
  directory and copy a SuperMongo plot macro as well as a plot script
  (svbplot) which parses the svb output file and feeds it to the
  SuperMongo script.

* You can create a small script file that runs svb and plots the
  results. This could look something like this:

> #!/bin/csh -f
> svb << EOT >& HV1333.log
> HV1333.in
> EOT
> svbplot HV1333.out
> cat HV1333.log

For svbplot to work properly you will need the scripts smgo, smgohard
and smgops which generates supermongo plots either on the screen, on the
printer or in a PostScript file or make your own equivalents. 


SuperMongo scripts:
===================

  For svbplot to run I have included the smgo, smgohard and smgops
scripts referred to above in the directory sm. Here you will also find a
graphcap file for sm with all the entries necessary for these three
scripts. The idea with the scripts is that they execute a SuperMongo
macro and sends the output in various formats either to the screen
(smgo) to the printer (smgohard) or to a PostScript file (smgops).

The smgo script requires that you specify the terminal type to
xterm (i.e. device xterm in your .sm file) for SuperMongo if running
on an X11 system, otherwise the plot will disappear immediately after
it has been produced or you might be able to fiddle with the script to
change the terminal type on the fly. 

Error estimates (Nov.97);
-------------------------
The linear regression/fitting is now done with the aide of the Isobe
et al. FORTRAN source code and the error estimates are computed
directly by this code as well (sixlin.f).
